		"nodes": {
			"1": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": true
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "TRIANGLE\nThis is the more complex version of the triangle shape with individually adjustable points. If you just need a simple triangle, make sure to check out the Equilateral Triangle node. Each point has a width and a height value that can be modulated individually. In this example we’ve taken full advantage of this by modulating each height and width value individually. This patch uses instancing. Consult the manual to learn about instancing.\n\n- Patch Explained -\n\n• The Linear node generates 6 values and outputs them as an array. We’ll be using these to offset the oscillators.\n• The array goes into a Split, this simply turns the array into separate float values.\n• The values are divided among the oscillators, offsetting their phases a bit. \n• Each oscillator is offset a different amount to make sure that the points don’t drift away too far.\n• The output of the Sines goes into a Pack and next into the point inputs of the triangle.\n• Finally, the Triangle goes into a Shape Render to convert the Shape2D to a texture.\n"
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 286.85711669921875,
					"width": 1068.867431640625,
					"x": 110.20876312255859375,
